A **true bearing** is the angle turned clockwise from north, written with **three digits** and a T, e.g. $047^{\circ}\text{T}$.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.3]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta,calc}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (1.802,-1.262);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0)--(0,2.3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0)--(0,-2.3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0)--(2.3,0);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0)--(-2.3,0);
\node at (0,2.62) {N};
\node at (0,-2.62) {S};
\node at (2.62,0) {E};
\node at (-2.62,0) {W};
\draw[thick] (O)--(A);
\fill (O) circle (1.6pt);
\fill (A) circle (1.6pt);
\node[below right] at (A) {$A$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] ($(O)+(90:0.85)$) arc (90:-35:0.85);
\node at ($(O)+(27.5:1.35)$) {$b^{\circ}$};
\node at (-0.42,0.42) {$O$};
\node at (-1.35,1.35) {$b^{\circ}\text{T}$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

A **compass bearing** turns from north or south through an **acute** angle towards east or west, e.g. $\text{N}47^{\circ}\text{E}$.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.3]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta,calc}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (1.802,-1.262);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0)--(0,2.3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0)--(0,-2.3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0)--(2.3,0);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0)--(-2.3,0);
\node at (0,2.62) {N};
\node at (0,-2.62) {S};
\node at (2.62,0) {E};
\node at (-2.62,0) {W};
\draw[thick] (O)--(A);
\fill (O) circle (1.6pt);
\fill (A) circle (1.6pt);
\node[below right] at (A) {$A$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] ($(O)+(-90:0.85)$) arc (-90:-35:0.85);
\node at ($(O)+(-62.5:1.5)$) {$a^{\circ}$};
\node at (-0.42,0.42) {$O$};
\node at (-1.35,1.35) {$\text{S}a^{\circ}\text{E}$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

The four quadrant conversions are $\text{N}a^{\circ}\text{E}=a^{\circ}$, $\text{S}a^{\circ}\text{E}=180^{\circ}-a^{\circ}$, $\text{S}a^{\circ}\text{W}=180^{\circ}+a^{\circ}$ and $\text{N}a^{\circ}\text{W}=360^{\circ}-a^{\circ}$.
